1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,358
A Zone operator selects the batch before an audit.

2
00:00:04,408 --> 00:00:08,766
Batch 006 has one withdrawal and two user transactions.

3
00:00:08,816 --> 00:00:11,705
They start Sworn's local proof job.

4
00:00:11,755 --> 00:00:17,583
It runs Tempo's code in SP1, then performs read-only verification on Moderato.

5
00:00:17,633 --> 00:00:23,950
The finished proof record can be sent to an auditor or settlement counterparty.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,450
Tempo's portal processes a withdrawal from a batch only after the Zone verifier accepts it.

7
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,950
So the operational dependency is simple: an operator, auditor,

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,950
or counterparty must be able to trust the batch check.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,394
This is the Operator Console in a browser.

10
00:00:45,444 --> 00:00:54,283
On a local workstation, the operator can start the real proof job for the supplied fixture.

11
00:00:54,333 --> 00:00:59,283
It takes about fifteen minutes and sends no transaction.

12
00:00:59,333 --> 00:01:03,172
The evidence panel then reads Moderato live.

13
00:01:03,222 --> 00:01:07,617
Its pre-T13 prototype verifier accepts a malformed batch.

14
00:01:07,667 --> 00:01:10,950
Sworn demonstrates the missing ZK check.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:19,063
Now Sworn, on a real batch from Tempo's integration tests: two user transactions and one withdrawal.

16
00:01:19,113 --> 00:01:26,668
Tempo's own batch verifier ran inside a zero-knowledge VM, and this contract checked the proof.

17
00:01:26,718 --> 00:01:29,711
Here it is on the explorer.

18
00:01:29,761 --> 00:01:32,753
Again, live: the real batch, true.

19
00:01:32,803 --> 00:01:36,809
Change one field, and it reverts: invalid proof.

20
00:01:36,859 --> 00:01:42,387
An equivalent malformed batch is accepted by Moderato's current prototype verifier,

21
00:01:42,437 --> 00:01:46,950
while Sworn rejects a mutation of its proven batch.

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:54,230
To be clear: this batch comes from a dev chain, not a Moderato Zone.

23
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:59,950
No portal uses it, and it does not protect withdrawals yet.

24
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,521
Near term: businesses that run Zones and answer to auditors,

25
00:02:04,571 --> 00:02:10,464
with evidence they match to each batch they settle, not yet a guarantee.

26
00:02:10,514 --> 00:02:16,407
Later, Tempo could build proofs into settlement; that design is written, not built.

27
00:02:16,457 --> 00:02:20,521
The service: provers on time, rebuilt at each upgrade.

28
00:02:20,571 --> 00:02:26,464
It's testnet and unaudited, with one Zone operator on Moderato and no customers.

29
00:02:26,514 --> 00:02:31,950
Next: one design partner, and a proof of a batch they supply.
